  "englishMetadata": {
    "title": "Game Score Design: Names, Units, and Comparisons",
    "description": "What does a game score evaluate? Compare cleared lines and evaluation scores in Tetris Effect: Connected, then examine card contributions, hand calculations, and round totals in Balatro.\n\nWhich selected cards actually contribute to the score? Distinguish the current hand result, round score, and target, then audit names, units, comparison references, and the order of feedback.\n\nThe actual gameplay portions use a 60:40 Balatro-to-Tetris balance. Alternating gameplay and spatial explanations on a black background, we explore score displays that help players choose their next action.\n\nChapters\n00:00 What does a score tell us?\n00:23 Quantity and evaluation\n00:52 Equal quantities, different scores\n01:18 Choose what to value\n01:49 The event and the running total\n02:18 Choose the comparison reference\n03:11 Give the number a name and unit\n03:41 Make the calculation readable\n04:12 Persistent values and transient notices\n04:44 Audit the score display\n05:23 Membership thanks\n\nGameplay recording: Squeaky Whale Gameplay Archive — https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCoMF_6EsJYSq8vWG8zqRvtA\n\n🎮 YamYamCoding helps you build practical game-programming skills through design and implementation.\n\n🚀 Premium one-to-one coaching\nDirectX 11/12, Unity, Unreal Engine, computer graphics, PBR, shaders, rendering, game engines, and graphics research implementation.\nhttps://www.yamyamcoding.com/1430b1ff-a61e-8040-a542-d672d5d25328\n\n💬 Community: questions, code reviews, feedback, and study materials\nDiscord\nhttps://discord.gg/wZuqe7fqkR\n\nYouTube membership\nhttps://www.youtube.com/channel/UCOgtkPoyC0VXhCs7Xk3jvjQ/join\n\n#GameDevelopment #GameDesign #GameScores\n",
